# The source distribution has to be able to REPRODUCE this package, not just
# import it.  setuptools' default file set carries the package and the
# metadata and nothing else, which would ship an sdist whose own README links
# resolve to files the reader does not have and whose test suite cannot run.
#
# Every graft below is a directory a document in this tree points at.

# The suite.  It runs against the INSTALLED engine wheel, never a checkout, so
# an unpacked sdist plus `pip install .[dev]` is a complete verification
# environment.  conftest.py is grafted with it: setuptools' default set picks
# up tests/test_*.py and leaves conftest behind, and a suite whose markers and
# fixtures went missing does not skip -- it errors, in a shape that reads as a
# broken package rather than a missing file.
graft tests

# The driver scripts.  They live outside the wheel on purpose (they are
# instruments, not library code) and the door pages name them by path.
graft tools

# The documents.  README and the door pages link into docs/ by relative path;
# without this graft every one of those links is a file the reader is missing,
# in a document that reads as though they should have it.
graft docs

# The workflows, so a reader can see what the release actually runs rather
# than take a checklist's word for it.
graft .github

# THE BREAKAGE THIS NAMES: the release notes were the one root document
# left off this list, and `tests/test_doors_bundle.py` reads them to hold
# the published Rust-door count to the door table.  So the suite that ships
# INSIDE the sdist failed from inside the sdist, on a FileNotFoundError for
# a file the reader was never given, which reads as a broken package rather
# than as a missing manifest line.  Measured 2026-09-07 running the CI
# selection from an unpacked sdist.
include LICENSE
include NOTICE
include README.md
include CHANGELOG.md
include RELEASE-NOTES-0.1.0.md
# Which revision of the model's source this package carries.  A reader
# of an sdist has the code and no way to place it without this.
include SOURCE.md
include pyproject.toml

# Compiled bytecode is a build product of the sources beside it, never an
# input; the working tree's own rule, applied to the artefact.
global-exclude *.pyc *.pyo
prune **/__pycache__

# Run output must never travel.  `graft tools` and `graft tests` are
# wildcards, and a wildcard is exactly how the thing nobody listed gets
# shipped: a checkpoint is 100s of MB, a receipt names the machine that wrote
# it, and neither helps anybody install or run this software.
prune out
prune runs
global-exclude *.npz
global-exclude *.nc

# THE ONE .npz THAT IS NOT RUN OUTPUT.  The hybrid covariance's static table
# is a shipped data file, estimated once and pinned by the receipt beside it,
# and `--static-covariance packaged` -- the DEFAULT, at beta 0.75 -- names it.
# The blanket exclusion above is right about every other .npz in this tree
# (checkpoints, analyses, ensemble members) and wrong about exactly this one,
# so it is named back in after the exclusion rather than by weakening it.
include src/arwen_global/data/static-covariance-v1.npz
